        <owl:annotatedTarget>Mushroom body neuron of the adult that bifurcates at the anterior end of the pedunculus, projecting into the mushroom body alpha&apos;-lobe and beta&apos;-lobe (Lee et al., 1999; Tanaka et al., 2008). It develops from a larval alpha&apos;/beta&apos; Kenyon cell with little alteration during metamorphosis (Lee et al., 1999). In the pedunculus, the alpha&apos; and beta&apos; neurons occupy an intermediate stratum between the gamma axons at the periphery and the alpha/beta neurons at the core (Tanaka et al., 2008; Li et al., 2020). Most arborize in the main calyx, with a subset arborizing in the lateral accessory calyx (Marin et al., 2020). There is a total of around 340 of these cells per hemisphere (Li et al., 2020).</owl:annotatedTarget>
